WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.790
Film Courage: How does a movie producer view
a screenplay versus how an actor, filmmaker

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.100
or writer views a screenplay?

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.990
Mark Heidelberger, Producer: Well since I’m
not an actor or some of these other people,

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:18.930
I can’t necessarily speak to their process
but I think a producer is probably a lot more

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:23.539
comprehensive in terms of when I look at a
script I’m thinking about a lot of different…I’m

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:26.279
thinking about all of those things.

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:33.470
Specifically how they relate budget wise and
also how the creative and the fiscal work

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:39.060
together so when I read the script I’m thinking
about story, I’m thinking about the arc

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.680
of the characters, I’m thinking about the
conflict, I’m thinking about why are we

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.260
telling this story but also who would I get
to play these roles on this budget level?

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.030
What are some decent names, who am I picturing
in this?

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:56.740
I’m thinking about…and I’m presuming
in this situation that the movie is real,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.420
that it’s not just a script, I’m not just
reading it for fun but it’s like Okay we’re

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.420
making this picture.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.810
Like this picture is funded Mark, read the
script.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:07.860
So I’m reading it and I’m like Okay how
are we going to pull off that?

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.990
We need this kind of grandiose house or a
good example would be there’s a project

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:20.470
I’m working on now called LI2 and very hopeful
that we’ll shoot this summer.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:27.830
The financing is getting locked down as we
speak and it’s a very cool thriller about

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:31.710
these two helicopter parents, these sort of
like wealthy parents.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:39.520
They’ve got these two ten year old twins
and the twins are kind of like…the parents

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.860
are finding them hard to tutor.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.280
The boy’s got like a learning disability
and the girl is kind of too smart for her

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.290
own good, too rebellious.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.640
And they’re having trouble tutoring these
kids and the kids have their own language

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.780
that they speak in when they don’t want
the parents to know what they’re saying.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.030
So the parents buy this advice call the LI2,
it’s kind of like an Alexa or a Google Home

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.520
like these smart devices, it connects to the
house, it can lock the door, it can open windows,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.960
it can do all of these these kind of things,
turn the light down, answer questions, whatever.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.340
And at first the kids don’t like it but
then it starts learning their language and

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:17.280
it befriends them and then the kids start
liking it and it starts tutoring the kids

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.480
better than the parents can.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.780
And eventually the kids start pushing back
on the parents a little bit and it starts

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.970
overstepping its bounds and the parents are
finally like “We need to get rid of this

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:26.970
thing.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.709
At first it’s good but then it starts to
take it a little bit over the line and by

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:36.500
this point the kids love this thing so much
that they secretly get another one without

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.920
the parents knowing and it secretly remembers
them, it’s mind is in the Cloud, it remembers

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.590
these two kids.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.130
And the parents decide we’re going to send
these kids to two different private schools

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.310
instead of together.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:46.310
The kids instead of together.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.840
The kids are like freaking out because they
are twins, they don’t want to be separated.&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.709
So the LI2 starts planning with the kids to
take out the parents, plotting with them to

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:56.690
get rid of the parents and it controls the
house and it locks the door.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.760
So this whole big…it builds to this really
big climax.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.360
It’s just a really big well-written script.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.739
So I’m using that as an example because
when I was reading the script and I was like

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:11.750
this is moving toward production, I’m looking
at this and going this house is such a major

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:12.750
location.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.680
We need to be…it’s like a character.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.370
The LI2, the voice of the LI2, what it would
look like, the lights on it, it glows or whatever,

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.580
is that going to be the practical effect,
is that going to be a post-visual effect.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.120
How are we going to pull that off.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.730
The casting, you’ve got the two parents,
you’ve also got the kids.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:34.970
When are you going to shoot this thing because
with kids you can only shoot so many hours.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:39.870
So with their age group maybe you can get
only 9 hours a day with them.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.530
If it’s during a school year, you can only
shoot for 6 of those hours.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.150
The other three they are having to be schooled,
the set teacher has to be schooling them.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:51.810
But if it’s in the summer when school is
not in (as long as the set teacher is on set)

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.989
we don’t have to burn shooting time with
school.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:57.630
Okay summer is probably a better time to shoot.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.450
I’m giving you some examples but production
design and I’m giving you some of these

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:07.050
things and I’m saying “We need this and
we need to dress the kids room like this.”

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:12.230
There’s a very specific style, do we know
somebody to keep this style, it’s very post-modern

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.989
I worked with another production designer
that did this and wow!

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:22.270
Sound is such a big deal, I’m thinking of
all these things as opposed to an actor reading

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:23.270
it.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:24.270
Yes, they are considering story.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:30.469
I think everyone with some sort of creative
position is thinking about story to some degree.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:33.639
But I think an actor is going to be much more
focused on their role, the arc of their role.&nbsp;

00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:36.360
And the cinematographer is going to be focused
on, they are going to be reading it and seeing

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.559
something totally different saying “Oh well,
so you’ve got a bunch of exterior night

00:04:40.559 --> 00:04:41.559
by the pool.

00:04:41.559 --> 00:04:45.409
Are we going to light the pool?

00:04:45.409 --> 00:04:47.949
I see that shot that follows the them through
the house.”

00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:51.539
So they are going to want to talk to the director
about how that…I’m thinking about all

00:04:51.539 --> 00:04:56.409
that but are we going to have the budget for
those kind of lights?

00:04:56.409 --> 00:04:59.870
Creatively if we can’t pull something off,
how are we going to be able to?

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:04.780
We can’t pull that off with the budget or
there is some other logistical circumstance

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.150
that is going to make that unreasonable, what
are we going to have to change to do that?

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:12.999
And I’m also a line producer and UPM so
I’m breaking down the script and I’m thinking

00:05:12.999 --> 00:05:13.999
about all of these things.

00:05:13.999 --> 00:05:16.289
Okay we need a steadicam to pull that off.

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:22.590
So we really do get down to that level of
detail to some degree but I’m thinking about

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:28.330
everything in one form or fashion as it relates
to creative and fiscal and how those two things

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:29.330
work together.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:34.499
I think writers are probably more focused
on story and a lot of them not necessarily

00:05:34.499 --> 00:05:35.919
on logistics.

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:38.659
When I talk to them I’m like “But how
are we going to pull this off?”

00:05:38.659 --> 00:05:41.030
And they’re like “Oh yeah?”

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.900
So from a producer’s perspective we’re
thinking more about execution.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:49.439
While I think a lot of writers they just tell
the story they want to tell and they don’t

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:52.210
necessarily think about how it’s going to
be done which is fine, you’re the writer

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.330
just get the story out but when it starts
to be come real, then we’re going to have

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.349
to start approaching how we’re going to
do it.

00:05:57.349 --> 00:05:59.759
Sometimes some things for logistical reasons
have to shift.&nbsp;

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:05.879
Film Courage: Do you think a lot of new writers
don’t see that just yet?

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:09.360
Once someone has been in the game for awhile
they can really scale things back to one room,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.360
one town?

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.659
Mark: You know I find the writers that grasp
it the quickest are multi-hyphenates who have

00:06:15.659 --> 00:06:16.659
also produced.

00:06:16.659 --> 00:06:21.569
Who have been involved in something other
than…you know a lot of times writers, they

00:06:21.569 --> 00:06:25.409
like locking themselves away in a room and
just working by themselves.

00:06:25.409 --> 00:06:27.789
They haven’t necessarily been part of the
collaborative, creative process.

00:06:27.789 --> 00:06:31.590
But the ones who have, the ones who have also
director, and also produced and understand

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:38.710
the extengies of production, I think those
people have a better grasp when I say “This

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:40.639
is going to be tough to do.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:44.849
Here are some ways we might be able to do
it given as an example I just recently budgeted

00:06:44.849 --> 00:06:48.809
a script called NOT LONG TOGETHER and I think
it’s fantastic and there was a scene in

00:06:48.809 --> 00:06:54.660
it (one bigger scene) where the guy shows
up, the woman is gone and there is an ambulance

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:58.869
and cop cars and there is a helicopter with
a spotlight because they are looking in the

00:06:58.869 --> 00:07:02.189
water and things and he’s like “Yeah,
we’ll probably have to get rid of that.”

00:07:02.189 --> 00:07:05.179
And I’m like “Well, there might be a couple
of ways that we can do this, a couple of cop

00:07:05.179 --> 00:07:09.809
cars, I know what they cost to rent or what
ambulances cost to rent.

00:07:09.809 --> 00:07:17.439
You can pay like $350 a day for picture vehicles,
caution tape and a few background actors walking

00:07:17.439 --> 00:07:21.529
through making it look busy” I said “You’re
biggest issue is your helicopter.

00:07:21.529 --> 00:07:27.550
But…we might be able to get a scissor lift
and a 5K or something and do the spotlight

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:32.129
and then license the stock footage shot of
a 4K stock footage shot of a police helicopter

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:35.520
and just cut to him.”

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.520
And they’re like “Yeah!

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:37.520
We can do it.”

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.029
Because the stock footage shot, you can license
that for $150 bucks.

00:07:40.029 --> 00:07:41.559
Go on Getty Images and you know?

00:07:41.559 --> 00:07:45.809
We did and we were like “Wow, look at all
these great 4K helicopter shots!”

00:07:45.809 --> 00:07:49.520
Cut that in and you can have your helicopters
and have a few cop cars and ambulances (whatever)

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:57.349
and it will probably cost us this much, we
can do it for $1,000…however many thousands

00:07:57.349 --> 00:07:58.349
of dollars.

00:07:58.349 --> 00:08:00.180
And they are like “Yeah!”&nbsp;

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:05.669
So coming up with let’s say creative solutions
sometimes it’s changes in the script, sometimes

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:07.709
it might not be how you’re thinking but
here is another way we can do it and that’s

00:08:07.709 --> 00:08:09.419
an example.

00:08:09.419 --> 00:08:10.419
And he was like “Yeah!

00:08:10.419 --> 00:08:11.419
This is awesome!

00:08:11.419 --> 00:08:11.421
Okay, great.”

